shdict.com/translate/estar%C3%A9is%20molando"}]}, "The search turned up one RAE source, but it is the 2001 edition, so the current definition still needs checking.\n\n- **RAE (DRAE 2001, 22.ª edición):** The archived entry lists *molar* as an intransitive verb, colloquial, with the sense of pleasing or being great. It notes the word comes from *caló*. The electronic version gives access to the text of the 22nd edition, published in 2001. The page points to the current edition, but that text did not come up in the results.\n- **Usage in Spain:** SpanishDict labels the verb colloquial and a Spain regionalism, with senses covering being cool, being fashionable, liking something, and, in negative sentences, not being able to stand something. Its example \"Ese móvil mola cantidad\" translates roughly as \"That phone is really cool.\" It is glossed as \"to be cool,\" and the example sentence is \"Ese móvil mola cantidad.\"\n- **Other senses:** Its noun use, *el molar*, is the tooth. The verb's slang sense is separate from the *molar* (adjective, \"relating to the molar tooth\") in the RAE entry.\n\nNext step: check the current DLE entry on rae.es for the up-to-date wording, since the 2001 text may differ."], "durationSeconds": 6.013240834, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01YaoqCCnC8wp1XQbn8wchbK", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
